#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184985} :activity/id #uuid "74cb5e70-9a9a-5b7d-b148-f583f043d560" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048506 :topic/id #uuid "a5765f97-9627-5a4e-ab27-f0369b4784bd" :topic/math-academy-id 3818} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184175 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184074 :question/id #uuid "edd4d491-c483-5004-b30f-68cf0ef76f43" :question/math-academy-id "e-5922"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d702975-166e-5067-ae3c-1152fade8248" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049970}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369707}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "b3f00d8e-e0e0-5507-a417-bc0bae247d7f" :step/math-academy-id 21956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184987}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184075 :question/id #uuid "04cb9ecc-68fa-546e-aa96-8a1ee050bbed" :question/math-academy-id "e-5923"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394752}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "1b0aff21-5c4b-587f-a636-9265b1edc074" :step/math-academy-id 21957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185333 :tutorial/content "One use for finding the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace is to find the shortest distance between the vector and the subspace.\n\nGiven a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and a subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ the **distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$** is given by \n\n$$\n\\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d10fd1778529183deb6912cebe9d2b7ad405091fe21a261c464d3d22c6d0860b.png)\n\nLet's find the distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x} = A(A^T\\!A)^{-1}\\!A^T\\,\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the distance formula, the distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-(-2))^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Recall that the vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" :tutorial/id #uuid "97ecd7fd-ac4d-5cca-86c7-db064dd69609" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6131 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "290e4663-8f35-58a4-8aae-e48323b5562f" :step/math-academy-id 37246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184986 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184111 :question/id #uuid "98aed663-0f72-5494-9a5f-ba2fdf94764a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049045}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369720}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "176d8f71-b7e6-5f7f-ace6-c9db9b253e57" :step/math-academy-id 37247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184987 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185334 :tutorial/content "As well as finding the distance between a vector and a subspace, we can also use the vector's orthogonal projection onto the subspace to find the angle between the vector and the subspace.\n\nGiven a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and a subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ the **acute angle between $\\mathbf{x}$ and $S$** is the acute angle formed by $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x},$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/1c/1c9fd00ab46691fefc65ffa6478ed589fbad1df6bfd19b913c6a4a94ae0358a0.png)\n\nLet's return to our previous example, where we had\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nPreviously, we found that the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ into the subspace spanned by $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can compute the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf x$ and $\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf x$ using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{||x||\\cdot ||{\\text{proj}}_{S}x||} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{20}\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3}{2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos\\left(  \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt5}\\right) \\approx 39.23^{\\circ},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle.\n\nNow, the acute angle between [math]\\mathbf{x}[/math] and [math]\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}[/math] is equivalent to the acute angle between [math]\\mathbf{x}[/math] and any non-zero vector that is parallel to [math]\\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x}.[/math] So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n[math]\\qquad[/math] [math]\\mathbf{x} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\end{bmatrix} \\quad[/math] and [math]\\quad \\dfrac12 \\, \\textrm{proj}_S \\mathbf{x} = \\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\end{bmatrix}.[/math] \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n\\begin{align}\n\\cos\\theta \n&= \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (-1) + 2 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{0^2+2^2+4^2}\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2+1^2}} \\\\[5pt] \n&= \\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{20}\\sqrt3} \\\\[5pt] \n&= \\dfrac{2\\cdot3}{2\\sqrt5\\sqrt3} \\\\[5pt] \n&= \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt5}.  \n\\end{align}" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c19ec4b-3bdc-50e9-aac0-193e2ab2c298" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6132 :tutorial/title "Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "53d452ce-ad18-557e-8201-0b1f1d4b9e5a" :step/math-academy-id 37248 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184986}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Euclidean Spaces (Arbitrary Bases): Applications" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184403}]}
